/* Legal & Trust Center — generated Wave B (2026-10-25) from master/legal/*.md after the pledge-only consistency fix. Every document is a DRAFT pending qualified legal review. */
window.GLX_LEGAL = [{"slug": "terms", "title": "Terms of Service", "html": "<h2>Terms of Service — GreenlitX</h2>\n<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> [Date to be inserted on adoption, after legal review] <strong>Status:</strong> Draft for legal review. Do not publish as final terms until a qualified attorney has reviewed and approved this document.</p>\n<h3>1. Who operates GreenlitX</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX is a film discovery and audience-participation platform operated by its individual founder as a Florida-based sole proprietorship.</p>\n<p>Throughout these Terms, &quot;GreenlitX,&quot; &quot;we,&quot; &quot;us,&quot; and &quot;our&quot; refer to that operator, doing business as GreenlitX. GreenlitX is a brand name only. It is not presented here as a limited liability company or corporation.</p>\n<p><strong>Operator legal details:</strong> GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<p>By accessing or using GreenlitX, you agree to these Terms of Service. If you do not agree, do not use the platform.</p>\n<h3>2. What GreenlitX is</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX allows visitors to:</p>\n<ul><li>Discover original film projects in development;</li><li>Record nonbinding pledge interest in projects they support — on GreenlitX a &quot;Green Light&quot; is expressed exclusively through recorded pledge interest; there are no free votes (see Section 6 and the Pledge and Funding Terms);</li><li>Follow projects and receive platform updates about them, where that feature is offered;</li><li>Watch completed films made available through the Watch Free section via third-party sources; and</li><li>Submit film projects or completed films for review, subject to the Submission Agreement.</li></ul>\n<p>GreenlitX may change, suspend, or discontinue any feature. Features described as planned, intended, or future are not promised and may never launch.</p>\n<h3>3. Film projects are in development — production is not guaranteed</h3>\n<p>Film projects presented on GreenlitX are in development unless a project page expressly states a different, verified status.</p>\n<p>Listing a project on GreenlitX does <strong>not</strong> mean that the project:</p>\n<ul><li>Is financed;</li><li>Is cast;</li><li>Is in production or post-production;</li><li>Has a distributor, release date, or release commitment; or</li><li>Will ever be produced, completed, or released.</li></ul>\n<p>Audience follows and pledge interest do not guarantee production. A project may be changed, paused, withdrawn, or abandoned at any stage.</p>\n<h3>4. Estimated budgets are not fundraising goals</h3>\n<p>Some project presentations may refer to an estimated production budget. An estimated budget is an early planning figure for what a production might cost.</p>\n<p>An estimated budget is <strong>not</strong>:</p>\n<ul><li>A fundraising or campaign goal;</li><li>An amount GreenlitX is seeking to raise;</li><li>An amount supporters have raised; or</li><li>A promise about how any future funds would be sufficient to complete a film.</li></ul>\n<p>Campaign goals, if any are set for a project in the future, are a separate figure. See the Funding Transparency page and the Pledge and Funding Terms.</p>\n<h3>5. AI-generated and concept materials</h3>\n<p>Posters, images, concept art, teasers, and other presentation materials on GreenlitX may include AI-generated or otherwise conceptual material.</p>\n<p>Concept materials illustrate an idea in development. They do <strong>not</strong> establish that:</p>\n<ul><li>Production activity has taken place;</li><li>Any person shown or named in concept material is attached to a project;</li><li>A location, set, cast, or crew exists for a project; or</li><li>A finished film will look or sound like the concept material.</li></ul>\n<p>Where a project page uses fictional billing or similar poster conventions as part of a concept presentation, that presentation must not be read as a statement of real cast or crew attachments.</p>\n<h3>6. Pledge interest — current status</h3>\n<p><strong>At present, pledges on GreenlitX are nonbinding pledge interest only.</strong></p>\n<p>That means:</p>\n<ul><li>No card is charged;</li><li>No payment method is collected or saved for a pledge;</li><li>Nothing is authorized, held, or reserved;</li><li>Recording pledge interest is not a payment, deposit, purchase, donation, or binding payment commitment; and</li><li>Pledge interest will <strong>never be silently converted</strong> into a payment, authorization, subscription, or binding commitment.</li></ul>\n<p>Any future payment feature would require a separate legal and payment-readiness process and a separate, explicit consent flow before any charge could occur. The full current and intended pledge rules are in the Pledge and Funding Terms, which form part of these Terms.</p>\n<p><strong>Stripe is not active on GreenlitX.</strong> GreenlitX does not currently process payments through Stripe or any other payment processor for film pledges.</p>\n<h3>7. Supporters are not investors</h3>\n<p>Following a project, recording pledge interest, or — if payment features are ever activated — making a conditional pledge does <strong>not</strong> make you an investor.</p>\n<p>GreenlitX offers no:</p>\n<ul><li>Equity or ownership interest in GreenlitX or in any film project;</li><li>Shares, securities, or investment contracts;</li><li>Dividends, interest, revenue share, profit participation, or other financial returns; or</li><li>Right to repayment or return based on a project&#x27;s commercial performance.</li></ul>\n<p>Nothing on GreenlitX is an offer to sell securities or a solicitation of an investment. Do not record pledge interest or, in future, make a pledge in expectation of profit.</p>\n<p>A pledge is also not presented as a tax-deductible charitable donation. GreenlitX is not presented as a charity.</p>\n<h3>8. Memberships are a separate product</h3>\n<p>Memberships, if and when offered, are a separate GreenlitX product from film pledges.</p>\n<ul><li>Paying for a membership, if memberships become available for purchase, would not itself fund a specific film unless the purchase page expressly says so in clear terms.</li><li>Recording pledge interest does not create a membership, and holding a membership does not create a pledge.</li><li>Membership features, prices, billing, renewal, and cancellation terms must be stated in the Membership Terms and at the point of purchase before any membership is sold.</li></ul>\n<p>See the Membership Terms.</p>\n<h3>9. Watch Free and third-party content</h3>\n<p>The Watch Free section presents completed films through embeds or links provided by third-party sources, such as public archives and video platforms. GreenlitX does not host all of those films itself.</p>\n<ul><li>Playback is subject to the third-party provider&#x27;s own terms and privacy practices.</li><li>A film&#x27;s availability may change at any time — a source may remove, restrict, region-limit, or change a film without notice to GreenlitX.</li><li>Inclusion in Watch Free does not mean GreenlitX owns the film or guarantees that it will remain available.</li><li>Watch Free films carry no pledge or fundraising feature unless a separate written agreement and a separate project page expressly provide otherwise.</li></ul>\n<h3>10. Accounts</h3>\n<p>Where registration is offered:</p>\n<ul><li>You must provide accurate account information and a valid email address.</li><li>You are responsible for keeping your password and account access secure and for activity under your account.</li><li>Accounts are for people aged 13 or older. Any future payment feature, if activated, will be limited to people aged 18 or older who are legally able to enter into the relevant payment agreement. [Age rules to be confirmed by qualified legal review.]</li><li>Guest follows, where offered, may be stored only in your own browser and may not be included in public counts. The Privacy Policy explains this in more detail.</li></ul>\n<h3>11. Green Light pledge interest and platform integrity</h3>\n<ul><li>A Green Light on GreenlitX is expressed exclusively through recorded pledge interest: there are no free votes. Where pledge interest is offered, one account may hold one active pledge-interest record per project, and the supporter may withdraw it.</li><li>You must not create multiple accounts, use bots or automation, or otherwise manipulate follows, pledge-interest totals, rankings, or counts.</li><li>GreenlitX may remove manipulated follows or pledge-interest records and may suspend or delete accounts used to manipulate the platform.</li><li>Public counts are intended to reflect real records. Displayed totals are aggregate figures only and do not identify who followed or recorded pledge interest.</li></ul>\n<h3>12. Your submissions and content</h3>\n<p>If you submit a project, a completed film, or other material to GreenlitX, the Submission Agreement applies in addition to these Terms.</p>\n<p>In summary:</p>\n<ul><li>Filmmakers retain their intellectual property, except for the limited rights expressly licensed to GreenlitX in the Submission Agreement.</li><li>You are responsible for what you submit and must have the rights needed to submit it.</li><li>Submission does not guarantee review outcome, publication, funding, production, or distribution.</li></ul>\n<h3>13. Intellectual property on the platform</h3>\n<p>The GreenlitX name and logo, the site design, and platform copy belong to GreenlitX or their respective owners and are protected by applicable law.</p>\n<p>Project materials belong to GreenlitX or the project&#x27;s rights holder, as applicable. Nothing in these Terms transfers ownership of any project, film, or platform material to a visitor, supporter, member, or submitter.</p>\n<h3>14. Acceptable use</h3>\n<p>You agree not to:</p>\n<ul><li>Break the law while using GreenlitX;</li><li>Infringe another person&#x27;s copyright, trademark, privacy, publicity, or other rights;</li><li>Harass, threaten, or target another user, filmmaker, or professional;</li><li>Impersonate any person, company, or GreenlitX representative;</li><li>Post or submit scams or misleading financial offers, including describing a GreenlitX pledge as an investment or promising returns on any project;</li><li>Upload malware or attempt to breach the platform&#x27;s security, accounts, or database access controls;</li><li>Scrape or access the platform in a way that burdens its operation; or</li><li>Use the platform to advertise unrelated products or services.</li></ul>\n<p>The Community Guidelines form part of these Terms.</p>\n<h3>15. Availability, disclaimers, and changes to the service</h3>\n<p>The platform is provided &quot;as is&quot; and &quot;as available,&quot; to the extent permitted by law. [Warranty disclaimer wording to be settled by qualified legal review.]</p>\n<p>GreenlitX may update features, project information, and these Terms. Material changes to these Terms will be posted on this page. Changes will not be applied to a recorded pledge interest in a way that converts it into a payment or binding commitment — any future payment would require separate explicit consent, as stated in Section 6.</p>\n<h3>16. Liability, governing law, and disputes</h3>\n<p>[TO BE SETTLED BY QUALIFIED LEGAL REVIEW: limitation of liability, indemnity if any, governing law — expected to be Florida, USA — venue, dispute-resolution and any arbitration or class-action provisions, and severability. No provision in this draft should be read as a settled choice on those matters.]</p>\n<h3>17. Ending your use</h3>\n<p>You may stop using GreenlitX at any time. Where the platform provides account or data-deletion controls, you may use them as described in the Privacy Policy.</p>\n<p>GreenlitX may suspend or terminate access for a violation of these Terms, the Community Guidelines, or applicable law.</p>\n<p>Ending your use does not create a payment obligation from pledge interest that was never charged.</p>\n<h3>18. Contact</h3>\n<p>For questions about these Terms: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<p>A monitored contact address for legal notices, privacy requests, copyright notices, and accessibility feedback must be published before launch. See the OWNER_INPUT_NEEDED section below.</p>\n<hr>\n<h3>OWNER_INPUT_NEEDED</h3>\n<p>The following placeholders must be supplied or confirmed by the owner, with legal review where noted, before this document — and the other Legal &amp; Trust Center documents that reuse the same placeholders — can be published as final:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Operator legal name</strong> — the founder&#x27;s legal name as sole proprietor, in the exact form counsel advises for public terms, the Stripe account (if ever activated), and legal notices. Currently: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com.</li><li><strong>Public contact address for the platform</strong> — a monitored GreenlitX email address for terms questions, privacy and deletion requests, submission withdrawals, accessibility feedback, and general legal correspondence.</li><li><strong>DMCA designated agent</strong> — name/title, email, and any postal address and telephone number to be published, plus confirmation of registration with the U.S. Copyright Office. See 06-copyright-dmca.md.</li><li><strong>Business / mailing address</strong> — whether any postal address will be published at all, and if so the exact address. No street address has been invented in these drafts; do not add one without owner confirmation and counsel&#x27;s advice.</li><li><strong>Governing law and dispute terms</strong> — Florida governing law, venue, and any arbitration/class-action position, as settled by counsel (Section 16).</li><li><strong>Age minimums</strong> — account age floor and the 18+ rule for any future payment feature, as settled by counsel (Section 10).</li><li><strong>Liability and warranty wording</strong> — limitation of liability and disclaimer language, as settled by counsel (Sections 15–16).</li><li><strong>Adoption date</strong> — the &quot;Last updated&quot; date to be inserted when the reviewed terms are adopted.</li><li><strong>Membership offer details</strong> — whether and when memberships will be sold, at what price, and with what benefits, renewal, and cancellation terms, before 04-membership-terms.md can be finalized.</li><li><strong>Future payment activation</strong> — written confirmation if and when Stripe is ever activated, Stripe gives any required approval for this model, and counsel approves the pledge consent flow. Until then, every document must continue to state that Stripe is not active and pledges are nonbinding interest only.</li></ol>"}, {"slug": "privacy", "title": "Privacy Policy", "html": "<h2>Privacy Policy — GreenlitX</h2>\n<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> [Date to be inserted on adoption, after legal review] <strong>Status:</strong> Draft for legal review. Do not publish as a final policy until a qualified attorney has reviewed and approved this document.</p>\n<h3>1. Who this policy covers and who operates GreenlitX</h3>\n<p>This Privacy Policy explains what personal information GreenlitX collects, how it is used, and the choices available to you.</p>\n<p>GreenlitX is operated by its individual founder as a Florida-based sole proprietorship, doing business as GreenlitX.</p>\n<p><strong>Operator / privacy contact:</strong> GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<h3>2. What we collect</h3>\n<h4>Account data</h4>\n<p>If you create an account, we collect:</p>\n<ul><li>Your email address; and</li><li>A display name you choose.</li></ul>\n<p>Passwords are handled by our authentication provider and are stored only as secure hashes by that provider. GreenlitX does not see or store your plain-text password.</p>\n<h4>Your activity on GreenlitX</h4>\n<p>If you use platform features, we store records of:</p>\n<ul><li>Projects you support by recording pledge interest (a Green Light is expressed exclusively through recorded pledge interest; there are no free votes);</li><li>Projects you follow;</li><li>Pledge interest you record — the project, the amount of interest recorded, its status, and the dates it was recorded, changed, or withdrawn; and</li><li>Membership records, if memberships are ever offered and you purchase one. No membership payment data is collected while memberships are not being sold.</li></ul>\n<h4>Things you send us</h4>\n<p>If you submit a film project, submit a completed film, register professional interest, or contact us, we collect the information and materials you provide in that form or message, including contact details you supply so we can reply and review the submission.</p>\n<h4>Guest data stored only in your browser</h4>\n<p>If you follow a project without an account, where that feature is offered, those choices may be stored only in your own browser&#x27;s local storage on your device. They are not sent to GreenlitX unless you later sign in on that browser and those choices are transferred to your account.</p>\n<h4>Payment data — none at present</h4>\n<p><strong>GreenlitX does not currently collect payment data.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Live charging is disabled and Stripe is not active.</li><li>No card is charged for a pledge, and no card number or payment method is collected or saved for pledge interest.</li><li>Recording pledge interest does not require payment details.</li></ul>\n<p>If payment features are ever activated in the future, payment methods would be collected and stored by the payment processor — not by GreenlitX — and this policy would be updated and published before that activation. See the Pledge and Funding Terms and the Funding Transparency page.</p>\n<h3>3. What we do not do</h3>\n<ul><li>We do not sell your personal data.</li><li>We do not rent your personal data.</li><li>We do not share your personal data with advertisers.</li><li>At the time of this draft, the site runs no advertising trackers and no third-party analytics. If that changes, the Cookie Policy and this policy must be updated first.</li></ul>\n<p>Embedded video players provided by third parties (for example, the Internet Archive or YouTube) are served by those providers and may collect data or set cookies under their own privacy policies. See Section 6 and the Cookie Policy.</p>\n<h3>4. How we use your information</h3>\n<p>We use the information described above only to operate GreenlitX, including to:</p>\n<ul><li>Create and secure your account and sign you in;</li><li>Show your follows and pledge-interest records in your account;</li><li>Display honest aggregate counts and totals (public pages show aggregates only — not who followed or recorded pledge interest);</li><li>Review and respond to submissions and professional-interest registrations;</li><li>Reply to messages you send us;</li><li>Send you service messages about your account, a project you follow, or pledge interest you recorded (for example, a material change in a project&#x27;s status); and</li><li>Protect the platform against fraud, manipulation of counts, abuse, and security incidents.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Marketing email:</strong> [TO BE SETTLED BY OWNER AND LEGAL REVIEW: whether any marketing email list will exist. If one is introduced, it must be opt-in only, and this policy must be updated before it launches.]</p>\n<h3>5. Where data lives and who processes it</h3>\n<p>Account, follow, pledge-interest, and submission records are stored in a database provided by Supabase, hosted in the United States. Supabase processes this data on GreenlitX&#x27;s behalf as a backend and authentication provider.</p>\n<p>Transactional account email — such as account-confirmation and password-reset messages — is sent through Resend, an email delivery provider, from accounts@greenlitx.com. Resend receives the email address and the message content needed to deliver those messages. Email you send to GreenlitX addresses (such as team@greenlitx.com) is received and stored in mailboxes provided by Zoho Mail. The public website itself is hosted by GitHub Pages.</p>\n<p>Database access rules are designed so that a signed-in user can read and write only their own records. Public pages show aggregate counts and totals only.</p>\n<p>If additional processors are added — for example, a payment processor if payments are ever activated — they must be listed in this policy before or when they are put into use.</p>\n<h3>6. Third-party embeds and links</h3>\n<p>Watch Free films are presented through third-party embeds or links. When you play or open third-party content:</p>\n<ul><li>The provider may collect information about your device, playback, and interaction under its own privacy policy;</li><li>The provider may set its own cookies or similar technologies — see the Cookie Policy; and</li><li>GreenlitX does not control the provider&#x27;s data practices.</li></ul>\n<p>Where a provider offers a privacy-enhanced embed mode, GreenlitX aims to use it.</p>\n<h3>7. How long we keep information</h3>\n<p>[TO BE SETTLED BY OWNER AND LEGAL REVIEW: a specific retention schedule for account data, pledge-interest records, submissions, and correspondence.]</p>\n<p>Until that schedule is adopted, the working principle is: keep personal information only for as long as it is needed to operate the platform, review submissions, maintain accurate aggregate records, and meet any legal obligations — and delete or de-identify it when it is no longer needed for those purposes.</p>\n<p>If payment features are ever activated, financial transaction records may need to be kept for a period required by law, tax, or payment-processor rules. That exception must be stated specifically when it becomes applicable.</p>\n<h3>8. Your rights and controls</h3>\n<p>Subject to applicable law, you may:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>See and correct</strong> your display name and review your follows and pledge-interest records in your account;</li><li><strong>Withdraw</strong> a follow or recorded pledge interest using the controls provided on the platform, where those controls are offered;</li><li><strong>Delete your own data</strong> using the account deletion controls provided on the platform, where offered, including deletion of follows, pledge interest, and profile information; and</li><li><strong>Request full deletion of your login and associated data</strong> by contacting: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</li></ul>\n<p>Full account deletion will be completed within [timeframe to be settled by legal review] of a verified request, except for any records GreenlitX is legally required to keep. Any such exception will be explained when it applies.</p>\n<p>Depending on where you live, you may have additional rights — such as access, portability, correction, deletion, or objection — under applicable privacy laws. Contact us using the details above and we will handle your request as required by the law that applies to it. [Applicable regimes and exact rights wording to be settled by qualified legal review.]</p>\n<h3>9. Children</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX accounts are for people aged 13 or older. Any future payment feature, if activated, will be for people aged 18 or older. [Age minimums and the platform&#x27;s position on children&#x27;s privacy law to be settled by qualified legal review.]</p>\n<p>GreenlitX does not knowingly seek personal information from children below the applicable account age floor. If you believe a child below that floor has provided personal information, contact us so it can be reviewed and deleted.</p>\n<h3>10. Security and breaches</h3>\n<p>Access to the platform database is controlled through authenticated sessions and access rules designed to limit each user to their own records.</p>\n<p>No system is perfectly secure. If a breach affecting your personal information occurs, GreenlitX will notify affected users and any authorities as required by applicable law.</p>\n<p>Do not send passwords, full card numbers, or other sensitive credentials to GreenlitX by email or through a submission form. GreenlitX does not ask for them for pledge interest.</p>\n<h3>11. Changes to this policy</h3>\n<p>We will post changes to this policy on this page. For material changes, we will notify account holders by email before the changes take effect, where an email address is held and notice is practicable.</p>\n<p>A change to this policy will not convert previously recorded pledge interest into a payment or binding commitment. Any future payment feature requires separate explicit consent.</p>\n<h3>12. Contact</h3>\n<p>For privacy questions, access requests, or deletion requests: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<p>A monitored contact address must be published before launch. Privacy and deletion processes in this policy depend on it.</p>"}, {"slug": "pledge-funding-terms", "title": "Pledge and Funding Terms", "html": "<h2>Pledge and Funding Terms — GreenlitX</h2>\n<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> [Date to be inserted on adoption, after legal review] <strong>Status:</strong> Draft for legal review. Do not publish as final terms until a qualified attorney has reviewed and approved this document.</p>\n<p><strong>Read this document before recording pledge interest. It states what a GreenlitX pledge is today, what it is not, and what would have to change before any payment could ever be taken.</strong></p>\n<h3>1. Current status — nonbinding pledge interest only</h3>\n<p>At present, a &quot;pledge&quot; on GreenlitX means <strong>nonbinding pledge interest</strong>.</p>\n<p>Recording pledge interest means only that you have told GreenlitX you would be interested in supporting a project for a stated amount if a lawful, fully disclosed conditional payment campaign is ever opened for that project.</p>\n<p>Right now:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>No card is charged.</strong></li><li><strong>No payment method is collected, saved, authorized, held, or reserved.</strong></li><li><strong>Nothing is owed.</strong></li><li>Recording interest is <strong>not</strong> a payment, a deposit, a purchase, a donation, or a binding payment commitment.</li><li>GreenlitX cannot charge you on the basis of pledge interest alone, and pledge interest will <strong>never be silently converted</strong> into a payment, authorization, subscription, or binding commitment.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Stripe is not active on GreenlitX.</strong> No payment processor is currently used to collect film pledges.</p>\n<h3>2. A pledge is not an investment</h3>\n<p>Whether recorded as interest today or — if a payment feature is ever activated — made as a conditional pledge in future, a GreenlitX pledge is <strong>not an investment</strong>.</p>\n<p>It gives you no:</p>\n<ul><li>Equity or ownership in GreenlitX or in any film;</li><li>Shares or securities of any kind;</li><li>Dividends, interest, revenue share, profit participation, or other returns;</li><li>Right to repayment based on a film&#x27;s performance; and</li><li>Ownership of a film, its copyright, or its distribution rights.</li></ul>\n<p>Nothing on GreenlitX is an offer to sell securities. Do not pledge in expectation of profit.</p>\n<p>A pledge is also not presented as a tax-deductible charitable donation, and GreenlitX is not presented as a charity.</p>\n<h3>3. Production is not guaranteed</h3>\n<p>Film projects on GreenlitX are in development. Recording pledge interest does not guarantee that a project will be financed, produced, completed, or released — even if strong interest is shown, and even if a future campaign goal were ever reached.</p>\n<p>A project may be changed, paused, withdrawn, or abandoned. See the Terms of Service and the Funding Transparency page.</p>\n<h3>4. Estimated budgets are not campaign goals</h3>\n<p>An estimated production budget shown or discussed for a project is a planning figure only. It is <strong>not</strong> a campaign goal and must not be read as the amount GreenlitX is raising for that project.</p>\n<p>Campaign goals are determined separately, as explained in Section 5. Until a campaign goal is expressly set and published for a specific project, that project has no campaign goal — its goal is <strong>to be determined</strong>.</p>\n<h3>5. How campaign goals would be determined</h3>\n<p>If conditional campaigns are ever opened:</p>\n<ul><li>A campaign goal would be set <strong>per project by the owner of GreenlitX</strong> and published on that project&#x27;s page before pledges with payment consent are accepted for that campaign;</li><li>A goal would <strong>never be auto-derived from a project&#x27;s estimated production budget</strong>;</li><li>Until a goal is set and published for a project, it remains &quot;to be determined&quot; and no payment pledge can be charged for that project; and</li><li>Any change to a published goal, deadline, or funding condition would be disclosed on the project page. A change would not be applied to take money from a supporter without that supporter&#x27;s consent under the consent flow in force at the time of any future charge.</li></ul>\n<p>The founding campaign deadline referenced in earlier platform planning must not be treated as a payment deadline for today&#x27;s pledge interest. Any binding campaign deadline would be stated expressly in a future, legally reviewed campaign offer.</p>\n<h3>6. The intended conditional pledge model — intention, not current operation</h3>\n<p>The model GreenlitX <strong>intends</strong> to use, if and only if the legal and payment gates in Section 7 are completed, is a conditional, all-or-nothing model:</p>\n<ol><li>A supporter chooses an amount and, at that future time only, saves a payment method with the payment processor through a secure payment flow and gives explicit consent to a single conditional charge.</li><li>Nothing is charged when the pledge is made.</li><li>The supporter is charged <strong>once</strong>, for exactly the pledged amount, <strong>only if</strong> the project reaches 100% of its published campaign goal on or before its published deadline.</li><li>If the goal is not reached by the deadline, nothing is charged and the pledge lapses.</li><li>&quot;Pledged&quot; (amounts promised) and &quot;collected&quot; (money actually received after a successful charge) are shown as separate figures. A pledged total is never presented as money in hand.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>This Section describes an intention for a future model. It is not how pledges operate today.</strong> Today, Section 1 governs: interest only, no payment method, no charge, no authorization.</p>\n<p>Details that are <strong>not yet established</strong> and must be settled by legal review before any activation include: how &quot;100% of goal&quot; is calculated if some charges fail, the length of any window to fix a failed payment method, and whether a collection shortfall after charges begin would unwind a campaign or allow it to proceed at the collected amount. Those mechanics must not be stated to supporters as fact until they are settled and published.</p>\n<h3>7. When a payment could ever be charged</h3>\n<p>No payment may be charged for a GreenlitX pledge unless <strong>all</strong> of the following have happened first:</p>\n<ol><li>Separate legal review of the pledge model, consent wording, disclosures, and cancellation and refund rules is complete;</li><li>Separate payment readiness is complete — including activation of a payment-processor account and any approval the processor requires for this type of platform;</li><li>The final funding terms, goal, deadline, and cancellation and refund rules for that specific campaign are published; and</li><li>The supporter completes a <strong>separate, explicit consent flow</strong> for that campaign — including saving a payment method with the payment processor at that time and agreeing to the exact amount, goal, deadline, and single-charge condition shown in that flow.</li></ol>\n<p>A database record of pledge interest, an account, a follow, or a membership is <strong>not</strong> that consent and will never be treated as that consent.</p>\n<p>Before any future charge, GreenlitX intends to remind the supporter of the project, the exact amount, and the upcoming charge window, and to provide a way to cancel or update the payment method before the charge runs. [Reminder timing and mechanics to be settled and published before activation.]</p>\n<h3>8. Withdrawing or cancelling pledge interest today</h3>\n<p>While pledges are interest only:</p>\n<ul><li>You may withdraw or cancel your pledge interest at any time using the controls provided in your account or on the project page, where those controls are offered, or by contacting GreenlitX at: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</li><li>Withdrawal is free. There is nothing to refund, because nothing was charged.</li><li>Withdrawn interest will not be charged, counted as an active pledge, or converted into anything else.</li></ul>\n<p>For any future payment pledge, a supporter would be able to cancel free of charge at any time before the campaign goal is reached and the charge runs. That cancellation right must be stated in the future consent flow. [Exact cancellation mechanics to be settled by legal review before activation.]</p>\n<h3>9. If a campaign goal is not reached</h3>\n<p>Under both today&#x27;s interest system and the intended conditional model:</p>\n<ul><li>If a project does not reach a published campaign goal by its published deadline, <strong>nothing is charged</strong>; and</li><li>Pledge interest or a future conditional pledge for that campaign lapses — it does not roll over automatically to another project, another campaign, or a later deadline.</li></ul>\n<p>GreenlitX will never quietly substitute a different project for the one a supporter backed.</p>\n<h3>10. Cancellation and refunds — intentions pending legal establishment</h3>\n<p>Because no payments are taken today, there are currently no pledge payments to refund.</p>\n<p>For a future payment model, GreenlitX&#x27;s <strong>intentions</strong> — stated here as intentions only, pending legal establishment and publication of final rules before any activation — are:</p>\n<ul><li>If GreenlitX cancels a funded project before production funds for that project are released, amounts charged for that project would be refunded in full to the original payment method;</li><li>Other refund situations, including a production failure after funds have been released, would be handled under a published refund policy settled by legal review before activation; and</li><li>Processing-fee treatment on refunds would be disclosed before activation, because a payment processor may not return its original processing fee when a payment is refunded.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Do not rely on this Section as an established refund policy.</strong> No refund mechanic in this Section is in force for a payment that has not been authorized under a future, legally reviewed consent flow. The final cancellation and refund rules must be published before any card is saved for a conditional pledge.</p>\n<h3>11. No extra calls for money for the same film</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX&#x27;s standing intention is that a project that reaches its campaign goal will not return to the same supporters for additional pledges for the same film. If a production encounters difficulty, that difficulty should be disclosed honestly to its supporters. [This intention to be confirmed as a binding rule by legal review before activation.]</p>\n<h3>12. Fees</h3>\n<p>No fee is charged to record, hold, withdraw, or cancel pledge interest today.</p>\n<p>Any future payment-processor fee, platform fee, or other deduction from collected funds must be disclosed in these terms and on the Funding Transparency page <strong>before</strong> payment activation. No undisclosed fee will be applied to a future charge.</p>\n<h3>13. Contact</h3>\n<p>For questions about pledge interest, or to withdraw it by email: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>"}, {"slug": "membership-terms", "title": "Membership Terms", "html": "<h2>Membership Terms — GreenlitX</h2>\n<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> [Date to be inserted on adoption, after legal review] <strong>Status:</strong> Draft for legal review. Do not publish as final terms until a qualified attorney has reviewed and approved this document. No membership price, tier, benefit list, billing cycle, or renewal term is stated in this draft, because none may be offered until the owner has confirmed the offer and it has been legally reviewed.</p>\n<h3>1. Memberships are a separate product</h3>\n<p>A GreenlitX membership, if and when offered, is a separate product from film pledges.</p>\n<ul><li>A membership is not a pledge to a film.</li><li>Recording pledge interest is not a membership, costs nothing, and creates no membership obligation.</li><li>Holding a membership, if memberships become available, would not by itself record a pledge or fund a specific film — unless a purchase page expressly and clearly states a specific funding allocation, in which case that statement and the Pledge and Funding Terms would both apply to that allocation.</li><li>Pledge interest remains nonbinding and uncharged regardless of membership status. See the Pledge and Funding Terms.</li></ul>\n<h3>2. Current availability</h3>\n<p>Memberships are <strong>not to be treated as available for purchase on the basis of this draft</strong>.</p>\n<p>Earlier platform material referred to founding memberships opening at a future date. That reference is a statement of intention, not an offer to sell a membership today, and it does not state a price or promise a benefit.</p>\n<p>No membership may be sold until, for that specific offer:</p>\n<ul><li>The price, what is included, the billing period, and any renewal terms are published on the purchase page and in an updated version of these Membership Terms;</li><li>The payment, cancellation, and refund terms for that offer are published; and</li><li>A working payment method for memberships is activated and disclosed.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Stripe is not active on GreenlitX.</strong> GreenlitX does not currently process membership payments through Stripe or any other payment processor.</p>\n<h3>3. A membership is not an investment</h3>\n<p>A membership, if offered, would be a purchase of the membership access or benefits expressly listed at the point of sale. It would give the member no:</p>\n<ul><li>Equity or ownership in GreenlitX or in any film;</li><li>Securities, dividends, interest, revenue share, or profit participation;</li><li>Right to financial returns from any film or from GreenlitX; and</li><li>Copyright or ownership in any film or project material.</li></ul>\n<p>Do not purchase a membership, if one is offered, in expectation of profit.</p>\n<h3>4. What a membership would include</h3>\n<p>[TO BE SUPPLIED BY OWNER BEFORE ANY SALE: the exact membership tiers, if any; the price of each tier; the billing period; and the exact benefits included in each tier.]</p>\n<p>Until that information is published:</p>\n<ul><li>No benefit should be assumed from general platform descriptions, project pages, or references to &quot;founding&quot; status;</li><li>Early or founding recognition, if offered, must be defined exactly at the point of sale; and</li><li>Access to view project pages, follow projects, use Watch Free, or record pledge interest must not be described as membership benefits unless the access actually requires a membership.</li></ul>\n<p>If a benefit cannot be delivered as described, that must be disclosed promptly. [Remedy for an undeliverable paid benefit to be settled by legal review before any sale.]</p>\n<h3>5. Billing, renewal, and price changes — to be established before any sale</h3>\n<p>No billing or renewal rule is in force under this draft.</p>\n<p>Before any membership is sold, GreenlitX must publish, at minimum:</p>\n<ul><li>Whether a membership is a one-time purchase or a recurring subscription;</li><li>If it is recurring: the amount and frequency of each charge, when the first renewal would occur, how renewal consent is obtained, and how to cancel renewal;</li><li>Accepted payment methods and the payment processor used;</li><li>When a price change would take effect and how members would be notified — a price change must not be charged to an existing member without the notice and, where required, the consent stated in the published terms; and</li><li>What happens to membership access if a payment fails.</li></ul>\n<p>No membership charge, renewal charge, or price change may be taken from pledge interest, a follow, or account registration. Any membership payment requires its own explicit purchase consent.</p>\n<h3>6. Cancellation and refunds — intentions pending legal establishment</h3>\n<p>Because memberships are not being sold under this draft, there are currently no membership payments to cancel or refund.</p>\n<p>GreenlitX&#x27;s <strong>intentions</strong>, pending legal establishment and publication of final rules before any sale, are:</p>\n<ul><li>A member would be able to cancel a recurring membership, if recurring memberships are offered, through their account or by contacting GreenlitX, and cancellation would stop future renewal charges;</li><li>Whether access continues to the end of a paid period after cancellation, and whether any period is refundable, would be stated plainly at the point of sale; and</li><li>If GreenlitX permanently discontinued a paid membership before delivering the paid period or benefit, a remedy — which may include a refund for the undelivered portion — would be published and applied.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Do not rely on this Section as an established membership refund policy.</strong> The final cancellation and refund rules must be published before any membership payment is taken.</p>\n<h3>7. Memberships and film projects</h3>\n<p>Membership does not guarantee that:</p>\n<ul><li>Any film project will be produced, completed, or released;</li><li>A member&#x27;s preferred project will be selected, funded, or prioritized; or</li><li>A member will receive a role, credit, employment, casting opportunity, or professional engagement in connection with a project.</li></ul>\n<p>Any credit, access, event, or other benefit connected to a specific project must be described exactly in the membership offer. General membership language must not be read as a promise of a screen credit or production involvement.</p>\n<p>Professional-interest registration is separate from membership and is not an offer of employment or casting. See the Submission Agreement where applicable.</p>\n<h3>8. Account and fair use</h3>\n<p>A membership, if offered, would be tied to the member&#x27;s own GreenlitX account and would be subject to the Terms of Service and Community Guidelines. Memberships must not be shared, resold, or used to manipulate counts or access controls unless the published offer expressly allows the stated sharing.</p>\n<p>GreenlitX could suspend membership access for a violation of the Terms of Service or Community Guidelines. [Effect of suspension on any amount already paid to be settled by legal review before any sale.]</p>\n<h3>9. Changes to these Membership Terms</h3>\n<p>Changes to these terms will be posted on this page. No change will convert pledge interest into a membership or a payment, and no change will impose a new membership charge on a person who has not agreed to purchase a membership under the changed terms.</p>\n<h3>10. Contact</h3>\n<p>For questions about memberships: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>"}, {"slug": "submission-agreement", "title": "Submission Agreement", "html": "<h2>Submission Agreement — GreenlitX</h2>\n<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> [Date to be inserted on adoption, after legal review] <strong>Status:</strong> Draft for legal review. Do not publish as a final agreement until a qualified attorney has reviewed and approved this document.</p>\n<p>This Submission Agreement applies when you submit material to GreenlitX. Part A covers original film projects submitted for possible publication. Part B covers completed films submitted for the Watch Free library. The general provisions in Part C apply to both.</p>\n<h3>Part A — Original film project submissions</h3>\n<h4>A1. You retain your intellectual property</h4>\n<p>You retain all rights in your submitted project — including copyright and all rights in its title, story, characters, script, artwork, and other materials — whether or not the project is reviewed, published, supported, or produced.</p>\n<p>GreenlitX claims <strong>no ownership</strong> of a submitted project, in whole or in part, under this Agreement.</p>\n<p>The only rights GreenlitX receives are the limited licenses expressly stated in Section A2. No other rights are granted by implication.</p>\n<h4>A2. The limited license you grant</h4>\n<p>By submitting a project, you grant GreenlitX a limited, non-exclusive, royalty-free license to:</p>\n<ul><li>Store, copy for internal review systems, and review your submission internally; and</li><li><strong>If — and only if — your project is approved for publication:</strong> display the materials you submitted for publication (such as the title, logline, synopsis, poster, and pitch materials) on the GreenlitX platform for as long as the project remains published.</li></ul>\n<p>This license does not give GreenlitX the right to produce your project, sell it, assign it, sublicense it for production, or use your materials for an unrelated project.</p>\n<p>If you withdraw your project under Section A6, the publication license ends and GreenlitX will remove the project from public view as described there. Copies already shared by visitors outside GreenlitX&#x27;s control (for example, a visitor&#x27;s own screenshot) cannot be recalled by GreenlitX.</p>\n<h4>A3. Review is human and publication is not automatic</h4>\n<p>Every project submission is subject to review before anything appears publicly.</p>\n<p>Submission does not guarantee:</p>\n<ul><li>Publication on GreenlitX;</li><li>Any particular audience response or level of pledge interest;</li><li>The opening of a funding campaign;</li><li>Financing, production, completion, or distribution; or</li><li>Employment, casting, or professional engagement for any person.</li></ul>\n<h4>A4. Your declarations</h4>\n<p>By submitting a project, you confirm that:</p>\n<ul><li>The project is your original work, or you hold all rights needed to submit it and to grant the license in Section A2;</li><li>The submission does not infringe another person&#x27;s copyright, trademark, privacy, publicity, or other rights;</li><li>Any real people, music, brands, artwork, footage, or existing works referenced or included are cleared by you to the extent clearance is required;</li><li>Any AI-generated material included in the submission is identified as such where the submission form asks, and the inclusion of AI-generated material does not by itself establish production activity; and</li><li>The information you provide — including development status and any proposed funding figure — is truthful and is not presented in a way that suggests the project is financed, cast, or in production when it is not.</li></ul>\n<p>You are responsible for what you submit.</p>\n<h4>A5. Funding is separate and conditional</h4>\n<p>Publication of a project does not open a payment campaign.</p>\n<p>Pledge interest in a published project is governed by the Pledge and Funding Terms: it is currently nonbinding, no payment method is collected, and nothing is charged. A proposed funding target supplied in a submission is not a published campaign goal. A campaign goal, if one is ever set, is set per project by the owner of GreenlitX and published separately — it is never auto-derived from an estimated production budget or from a figure in a submission.</p>\n<h4>A6. Withdrawal</h4>\n<p>You may withdraw a project submission or a published project at any time by contacting GreenlitX at: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<p>GreenlitX will remove a withdrawn published project from public view within [timeframe to be settled by legal review] of a verified request.</p>\n<p>Pledge interest recorded for a withdrawn project will be treated as lapsed and will never be charged. Under any future payment model, pledges recorded but not yet charged for a withdrawn project would be cancelled and never charged.</p>\n<h4>A7. No submission fee at present</h4>\n<p>GreenlitX currently charges no submission or listing fee for project submissions.</p>\n<p>Any future submission, listing, or platform fee must be disclosed in this Agreement and — where it would affect collected funds — in the Pledge and Funding Terms and Funding Transparency page before it is charged.</p>\n<h4>A8. No restriction on your other work</h4>\n<p>Nothing in this Agreement restricts you from pitching, developing, financing, or producing your project anywhere else, at any time, including while it is submitted to or published on GreenlitX, unless you sign a separate written agreement that expressly says otherwise.</p>\n<h3>Part B — Completed-film submissions for Watch Free</h3>\n<h4>B1. Who may submit</h4>\n<p>Only a film&#x27;s rights holder, or a person authorized by the rights holder to act for them, may submit a completed film for Watch Free. By submitting, you confirm that you are one of those people.</p>\n<h4>B2. You retain your intellectual property</h4>\n<p>You retain all rights in your completed film. GreenlitX claims no ownership of the film under this Agreement.</p>\n<p>The only permission GreenlitX receives is the limited permission expressly stated in Section B3.</p>\n<h4>B3. The limited permission you grant</h4>\n<p>By submitting a completed film, you grant GreenlitX permission to:</p>\n<ul><li>List the film in the Watch Free library with the credits and details you supply; and</li><li>Present the film to visitors through an embed of the film&#x27;s existing authorized copy on its source platform (for example, an official video-platform upload or an archive item you control).</li></ul>\n<p>GreenlitX does not, under this permission, download, copy, re-host, edit, or sell your film.</p>\n<h4>B4. The source copy stays in control</h4>\n<p>The embed keeps the source provider&#x27;s player, controls, branding, and attribution.</p>\n<p><strong>Availability may change.</strong> If you remove, restrict, or region-limit the source copy, the GreenlitX presentation may stop working and the listing will be removed. GreenlitX does not guarantee that a Watch Free film will remain available for any period.</p>\n<h4>B5. Your declarations</h4>\n<p>By submitting a completed film, you confirm that:</p>\n<ul><li>The film is complete;</li><li>The credits and details you supply are accurate;</li><li>The source copy is lawfully available for free viewing and embedding in the territories where it is offered; and</li><li>The listing basis is accurately described — for example, public domain, an open license, or rights-holder authorization — and any license conditions will be followed.</li></ul>\n<h4>B6. Revocation</h4>\n<p>You may revoke Watch Free permission at any time by contacting GreenlitX at: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<p>GreenlitX will remove the listing within [timeframe to be settled by legal review — working draft in earlier platform material suggested 5 business days] of a verified request. Revocation is not retroactive and does not by itself create a claim over views that already occurred while the listing was authorized.</p>\n<h4>B7. No payment and no exclusivity</h4>\n<p>Watch Free listing is free and non-exclusive. GreenlitX pays no fee for a Watch Free listing under this Agreement and claims no rights in the film beyond Section B3.</p>\n<p>A Watch Free film carries no pledge or fundraising feature unless a separate written agreement expressly provides otherwise.</p>\n<h3>Part C — General provisions</h3>\n<h4>C1. Materials you submit through other forms</h4>\n<p>Professional-interest registrations and other platform forms are submissions of information for review only. Registering professional interest is not an application outcome, an offer of employment, a casting decision, or a promise of engagement. Any personal information in a submission is handled under the Privacy Policy.</p>\n<h4>C2. Removal by GreenlitX</h4>\n<p>GreenlitX may decline, remove, or restrict a submission or listing that:</p>\n<ul><li>Violates the Terms of Service or Community Guidelines;</li><li>Is the subject of a copyright notice handled under the Copyright and DMCA Policy;</li><li>Contains information GreenlitX reasonably believes to be false about rights, development status, financing, or production; or</li><li>Creates a legal or security risk for the platform.</li></ul>\n<p>Removal of a project does not transfer any rights in it to GreenlitX.</p>\n<h4>C3. No partnership or representation</h4>\n<p>Submitting material to GreenlitX does not create a partnership, agency, employment, joint-venture, or representation relationship between you and GreenlitX.</p>\n<h4>C4. Changes to this Agreement</h4>\n<p>Changes to this Agreement will be posted on this page. A change will not expand GreenlitX&#x27;s rights in a project or film you have already submitted beyond the license or permission you granted under the version in force at submission, unless you agree to the expanded right in writing.</p>\n<h4>C5. Contact</h4>\n<p>For submission questions, withdrawals, or Watch Free revocations: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>"}, {"slug": "copyright-dmca", "title": "Copyright and DMCA", "html": "<h2>Copyright and DMCA Policy — GreenlitX</h2>\n<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> [Date to be inserted on adoption, after legal review] <strong>Status:</strong> Draft for legal review. Do not publish as a final policy until a qualified attorney has reviewed and approved this document and the designated agent details in Section 3 have been completed and registered as required.</p>\n<h3>1. GreenlitX respects copyright</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX respects the copyright and other intellectual-property rights of filmmakers and other rights holders.</p>\n<ul><li>Original film projects are published only after the submitter has given the rights declarations required by the Submission Agreement.</li><li>The Watch Free library is intended to list only films that are public domain, openly licensed (for example, under a Creative Commons license whose conditions are followed), or included with the rights holder&#x27;s authorization. GreenlitX aims to keep a verification record of each Watch Free title&#x27;s source and listing basis.</li><li>Filmmakers retain their intellectual property. GreenlitX claims no ownership of a submitted project or a completed film except under a separate written agreement that expressly says otherwise.</li></ul>\n<p>Mistakes can still happen. This policy explains how to report one and how GreenlitX will respond.</p>\n<h3>2. Third-party and embedded content</h3>\n<p>Many Watch Free films are presented through an embed of a copy hosted by a third-party provider. Removing a GreenlitX listing ends the presentation on GreenlitX; it does not remove the source copy from the provider&#x27;s own service. A rights holder may also need to contact the source provider about the source copy itself.</p>\n<p>Availability of third-party content may change at any time. Removal of a listing under this policy does not create a right to keep infringing or unavailable content online.</p>\n<h3>3. Designated copyright agent</h3>\n<p>Copyright notices under this policy should be sent to GreenlitX&#x27;s designated agent:</p>\n<p><strong>Designated agent:</strong> GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<p>[TO BE COMPLETED BEFORE LAUNCH: designated agent name or title, monitored email address, and any postal address and telephone number to be published. A DMCA designated agent must also be registered with the U.S. Copyright Office before launch. No agent details are stated in this draft because none have been confirmed for publication.]</p>\n<p>Email alone should not be assumed to be a monitored takedown channel until a specific takedown address is published in this Section.</p>\n<h3>4. How to send a takedown notice</h3>\n<p>If you believe content on GreenlitX infringes your copyright, send a notice to the designated agent in Section 3 that includes:</p>\n<ol><li>Identification of the copyrighted work you own or are authorized to act for — its title and, if available, registration details;</li><li>The exact GreenlitX page URL where the allegedly infringing content appears;</li><li>Your full name, postal address, telephone number, and email address;</li><li>A statement that you have a good-faith belief that the use of the material is not authorized by the copyright owner, the owner&#x27;s agent, or the law;</li><li>A statement, under penalty of perjury, that the information in your notice is accurate and that you are the copyright owner or are authorized to act on the owner&#x27;s behalf; and</li><li>Your physical or electronic signature.</li></ol>\n<p>An incomplete notice may delay review. GreenlitX may ask for the missing information needed to assess the claim.</p>\n<p>Do not send passwords, full payment-card numbers, or other unrelated sensitive information in a copyright notice.</p>\n<h3>5. What GreenlitX does after a complete notice</h3>\n<p>After receiving a complete notice, GreenlitX will:</p>\n<ul><li>Review the notice promptly;</li><li>Remove or disable access to the identified content while the claim is assessed — for an embedded Watch Free film, removing the listing ends the presentation on GreenlitX; and</li><li>Notify the submitter or uploader of the content, where GreenlitX holds their contact details and notification is appropriate.</li></ul>\n<p>Removal under this policy is not a final judicial determination that infringement occurred. It is a step in the notice-and-takedown process described here.</p>\n<h3>6. Counter-notice</h3>\n<p>If your content was removed or disabled under this policy and you believe it was removed by mistake or misidentification, or that you hold the rights needed for its use, you may send a counter-notice to the designated agent in Section 3 including:</p>\n<ul><li>Your full name, postal address, telephone number, and email address;</li><li>The GreenlitX URL of the removed or disabled content and a description of it;</li><li>A statement, under penalty of perjury, that you have a good-faith belief the content was removed or disabled as a result of mistake or misidentification;</li><li>A statement consenting to the jurisdiction of the federal district court for the district in which your address is located or, if you are outside the United States, the U.S. District Court for the Southern District of Florida, and that you will accept service of process from the person who sent the original notice or their agent; and</li><li>Your physical or electronic signature.</li></ul>\n<p>If GreenlitX receives a valid counter-notice, it may restore the content no sooner than 10 and no later than 14 business days after receiving the counter-notice, unless the person who sent the original notice first informs GreenlitX that they have filed a court action seeking to restrain the allegedly infringing activity. [Procedure to be confirmed against 17 U.S.C. § 512(g) by qualified legal review.]</p>\n<h3>7. Repeat infringers</h3>\n<p>Accounts and submitters who repeatedly infringe copyright or other intellectual-property rights will have their accounts or submission privileges terminated and may be barred from resubmitting. GreenlitX will apply this repeat-infringer rule in appropriate circumstances, as required for the takedown process it operates under.</p>\n<h3>8. Other intellectual-property complaints</h3>\n<p>For a trademark or other intellectual-property concern that is not a copyright claim under Section 4, contact GreenlitX at: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<p>Include the same practical details — what right is involved, the exact GreenlitX URL, and how to reach you — so the report can be assessed.</p>\n<h3>9. No advice to submitters</h3>\n<p>This policy explains GreenlitX&#x27;s reporting process. It is not legal advice to a rights holder, submitter, or visitor about whether particular material infringes or is licensed. If you are unsure about your rights, consult a qualified attorney.</p>"}, {"slug": "community-guidelines", "title": "Community Guidelines", "html": "<h2>Community Guidelines — GreenlitX</h2>\n<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> [Date to be inserted on adoption, after legal review]</p>\n<h3>1. What GreenlitX is for</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX is a place for people who love film to discover projects in development, support the projects they believe in honestly, watch lawfully available films, and connect around the work.</p>\n<p>These Guidelines apply to accounts, submissions, professional-interest registrations, and any comments or other community features GreenlitX offers now or in the future. They form part of the Terms of Service.</p>\n<h3>2. Do</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Be honest</strong> — about who you are, what you made, what stage it is really at, and what rights you hold.</li><li><strong>Follow projects and record pledge interest only for yourself</strong>, using your own account.</li><li><strong>Credit other people&#x27;s work</strong> when you reference, quote, or build on it.</li><li><strong>Keep feedback about the work</strong> — disagree with a project, a decision, or an opinion without targeting a person.</li><li><strong>Describe projects accurately.</strong> A project in development must not be presented as financed, cast, in production, or guaranteed to be made when it is not.</li><li><strong>Respect availability and rights in Watch Free.</strong> If you submit or share a film, make sure it is lawfully available for that use.</li></ul>\n<h3>3. Do not</h3>\n<ul><li>Harass, threaten, stalk, or target another user, filmmaker, professional, or GreenlitX representative.</li><li>Post hate speech or content that attacks a person or group on a protected characteristic.</li><li>Post sexual content involving minors, or any child sexual abuse material. Such content will be removed and may be reported as required by law.</li><li>Post graphic content that has no legitimate connection to a film&#x27;s presentation.</li><li>Impersonate another person, filmmaker, company, professional, or GreenlitX representative, or misrepresent your affiliation with a project.</li><li>Manipulate GreenlitX&#x27;s numbers in any way — including multiple accounts, bots, automated manipulation, coordinated inflation, or artificial pledge interest. Counts and totals on GreenlitX must reflect real people and real records.</li><li>Spam, advertise unrelated products, or flood submissions, forms, or community features.</li><li>Post scams or misleading financial offers — including promising investment returns on any project, describing a pledge as an investment or security, or suggesting that supporting a project will make anyone money. <strong>GreenlitX pledges are not investments. A pledge on GreenlitX is nonbinding pledge interest only — no money is collected, no card is charged, and nothing is owed.</strong></li><li>Claim that AI-generated or concept material proves a film is in production, or present concept material as real production footage, casting, or locations when it is not.</li><li>Submit or post material you do not have the rights to use, or ask others to infringe copyright for you.</li><li>Attempt to access another user&#x27;s account or data, probe or breach the platform&#x27;s security, upload malware, or interfere with the platform&#x27;s operation.</li><li>Collect other users&#x27; personal information or publish another person&#x27;s private contact details, address, or other private information.</li></ul>\n<h3>4. Submissions and professional interest</h3>\n<ul><li>A submission must follow the Submission Agreement, including its rights declarations.</li><li>A professional-interest registration is an expression of interest only. It is not a job offer, a casting decision, a promise of an interview, or a guarantee of work — and it must not be represented to anyone else as one.</li><li>Do not submit another person&#x27;s project, film, or personal details without their authorization.</li></ul>\n<h3>5. Enforcement</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX may, depending on severity and with or without prior warning:</p>\n<ul><li>Remove or restrict content, a submission, or a listing;</li><li>Cancel manipulated follows or pledge-interest records;</li><li>Refuse or close a submission or professional-interest registration; and</li><li>Suspend or delete an account.</li></ul>\n<p>GreenlitX may also preserve records and cooperate with a lawful request where required in connection with serious abuse, fraud, or illegal content.</p>\n<h3>6. If you think enforcement was wrong</h3>\n<p>If you believe GreenlitX removed your content or restricted your account by mistake, contact: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<p>Include your account email, the content or action involved, and why you believe it was a mistake. A report or appeal does not guarantee restoration.</p>\n<h3>7. Reporting a problem</h3>\n<p>To report harassment, manipulation, a rights problem, or other abuse, contact: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<p>For a copyright claim, use the formal notice process in the Copyright and DMCA Policy instead of a general report — a takedown notice must contain the specific statements listed there.</p>"}, {"slug": "cookie-policy", "title": "Cookie Policy", "html": "<h2>Cookie Policy — GreenlitX</h2>\n<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> [Date to be inserted on adoption, after legal review] <strong>Status:</strong> Draft for legal review. The technology descriptions in this policy must be checked against the live site&#x27;s actual implementation immediately before publication, and updated whenever a new tracker, analytics tool, or payment feature is added.</p>\n<h3>1. What cookies and similar technologies are</h3>\n<p>Cookies are small files a website or provider stores in your browser. GreenlitX and its providers may also use similar technologies, including browser local storage, which stores information on your device so the site can remember a choice or keep you signed in.</p>\n<p>This policy covers cookies, local storage, and similar device storage together, except where a section distinguishes them.</p>\n<h3>2. What GreenlitX itself uses</h3>\n<h4>Essential account and security storage</h4>\n<p>If you create an account or sign in, our authentication and backend provider may store session and security information in your browser so that:</p>\n<ul><li>You stay signed in;</li><li>Your account session can be secured and refreshed; and</li><li>The platform can confirm that account actions come from your signed-in session.</li></ul>\n<p>These are necessary for an account to work. Blocking them may prevent sign-in or account features from functioning.</p>\n<h4>Your own choices stored in your browser</h4>\n<p>Where GreenlitX offers guest follows, display preferences, or reduced-motion or similar presentation choices, those choices may be stored in your browser&#x27;s local storage on your device.</p>\n<p>Guest follows stored this way are <strong>not sent to GreenlitX</strong> unless you later sign in on that browser and they are transferred to your account, as described in the Privacy Policy. Clearing your browser storage will normally delete these locally stored choices.</p>\n<h4>What GreenlitX does not currently use</h4>\n<p>At the time of this draft:</p>\n<ul><li>GreenlitX runs <strong>no advertising trackers</strong>;</li><li>GreenlitX runs <strong>no third-party analytics</strong>; and</li><li><strong>Stripe is not active</strong>, so no payment-processor cookies or payment storage are set for film pledges or memberships, because no payments are currently processed.</li></ul>\n<p>If any of this changes, this policy and the Privacy Policy must be updated and published before or when the new technology is activated. A future payment page, if payments are ever activated, would involve the payment processor&#x27;s own cookies or similar technologies under that processor&#x27;s policies — that must be disclosed at that time.</p>\n<h3>3. Third-party embeds — Watch Free and video providers</h3>\n<p>Watch Free films and other embedded media are served by third-party providers, such as the Internet Archive and YouTube.</p>\n<p>When an embedded player loads or you press play, the provider may:</p>\n<ul><li>Set its own cookies or use similar storage;</li><li>Collect playback, device, and interaction information; and</li><li>Process that information under its own privacy and cookie policies, not GreenlitX&#x27;s.</li></ul>\n<p>GreenlitX does not control a third-party provider&#x27;s cookies. Where a provider offers a privacy-enhanced embed mode, GreenlitX aims to use it.</p>\n<p>If you do not want a provider&#x27;s cookies, do not play that provider&#x27;s embedded content, and use your browser settings to block or clear third-party cookies. Blocking them may prevent playback.</p>\n<p>Links that take you off GreenlitX are governed by the destination site&#x27;s own policies.</p>\n<h3>4. Managing cookies and browser storage</h3>\n<p>You can control cookies and local storage through your browser settings, including by:</p>\n<ul><li>Blocking all or third-party cookies;</li><li>Deleting cookies and site data for GreenlitX or for a specific provider;</li><li>Clearing local storage; and</li><li>Using private or incognito browsing.</li></ul>\n<p>The exact steps differ by browser — check your browser&#x27;s help pages. If you block or delete essential account storage, sign-in and account features may not work. If you clear local storage, guest follows and preferences saved only on your device will be lost.</p>\n<p>Any cookie-consent banner or preference tool GreenlitX uses in the future must be described in this Section before it is relied on. [Consent mechanism, if any is required for the technologies actually in use, to be settled by legal review and verified against the live build.]</p>\n<h3>5. Changes to this policy</h3>\n<p>We will post changes on this page. Adding an advertising tracker, an analytics provider, or a payment processor is a material change for this policy and must not happen without updating this policy first.</p>\n<h3>6. Contact</h3>\n<p>For questions about this Cookie Policy or the Privacy Policy: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>"}, {"slug": "accessibility", "title": "Accessibility", "html": "<h2>Accessibility Statement — GreenlitX</h2>\n<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> [Date to be inserted on adoption, after legal review]</p>\n<h3>1. Our commitment</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX wants the platform to be usable by everyone, including people who use assistive technology.</p>\n<p>Accessibility is an ongoing build standard for GreenlitX, not a one-time claim. This statement describes what GreenlitX aims to build in, what is known to be limited, and how to ask for help or an alternative way to access content.</p>\n<h3>2. Target standard</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX aims to design and test the platform against <strong>WCAG 2.1 Level AA</strong> as its working target. [Target standard and any audit plan to be confirmed by owner with legal review.]</p>\n<p>GreenlitX has <strong>not</strong> had an independent accessibility audit or WCAG conformance certification, and this statement does not claim one. This statement must not be read as a guarantee that every page conforms to the target standard at all times.</p>\n<h3>3. What we build in</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX&#x27;s build standards include:</p>\n<ul><li>Semantic page structure with headings and landmarks;</li><li>Labeled form fields and form error information that does not rely on color alone;</li><li>Visible keyboard focus states and keyboard-operable controls;</li><li>Color contrast checked for body text against the platform&#x27;s dark theme, with bright accent colors reserved for highlights rather than relied on for small essential text;</li><li>Support for reduced-motion preferences — animated showcase and effects content stops or simplifies when a visitor&#x27;s system requests reduced motion;</li><li>Responsive layouts that work at phone sizes and at zoom, with touch targets sized for phones, where most of the audience watches; and</li><li>Text alternatives or labels for meaningful images and controls, where the content is GreenlitX&#x27;s own.</li></ul>\n<p>These are design and build practices. An individual page or a newly added feature may still contain a barrier — Section 5 explains how to report one.</p>\n<h3>4. Known limitations</h3>\n<h4>Third-party players and films</h4>\n<p>Video playback uses established third-party players (for example, the Internet Archive and YouTube). Captions, audio description, transcripts, and player accessibility features depend on what the provider and the film&#x27;s source copy offer.</p>\n<p>GreenlitX cannot add captions or audio description to a film it does not host. Many older and public-domain titles predate captioning, and their source copies may have no captions available.</p>\n<h4>Project and concept materials</h4>\n<p>Posters and concept images are visual by nature. GreenlitX aims to provide a text description of a project&#x27;s story and status in the project presentation alongside its artwork, so the essential information about a project does not exist only inside an image.</p>\n<h4>Availability of third-party content</h4>\n<p>A Watch Free film may be removed or restricted by its source provider. If an accessible version of the same film is lawfully available from the source, GreenlitX will consider linking or embedding that version on request — GreenlitX cannot create or host an accessible copy it has no right to make.</p>\n<h3>5. Feedback and alternative access</h3>\n<p>If something on GreenlitX is hard or impossible for you to use, contact: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<p>Please include:</p>\n<ul><li>The page URL;</li><li>What you were trying to do;</li><li>The assistive technology, browser, or device you were using, if you are willing to share it; and</li><li>How to reach you.</li></ul>\n<p>GreenlitX will work on a fix or, where a fix cannot be made promptly, offer an alternative way to get the same essential content or complete the same action — for example, providing project information in text form or accepting a follow, pledge-interest record, or submission through a contact route instead of an inaccessible form.</p>\n<p>Accessibility feedback must go to a monitored address. That address must be published in this Section before launch.</p>\n<h3>6. Changes to this statement</h3>\n<p>We will update this statement as accessibility work is completed, as known limitations change, and if an independent audit is carried out. Updates will be posted on this page with a revised date.</p>"}, {"slug": "funding-transparency", "title": "Funding Transparency", "html": "<h2>Funding Transparency — GreenlitX</h2>\n<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> [Date to be inserted on adoption, after legal review] <strong>Status:</strong> Draft for legal review. Do not publish as a final transparency page until a qualified attorney has reviewed and approved this document.</p>\n<p>This page explains, in plain language, how money is — and is not — involved in GreenlitX today, how GreenlitX intends a future conditional pledge model to work, and how GreenlitX intends to earn revenue. Where something is an intention rather than an established process, this page says so.</p>\n<h3>1. The short version — where things stand today</h3>\n<ul><li>Film projects on GreenlitX are <strong>in development</strong>. Production is <strong>not guaranteed</strong>, even for a popular project.</li><li><strong>Pledges today are nonbinding pledge interest only.</strong> No card is charged, no payment method is collected or saved, nothing is authorized, and nothing is owed.</li><li>Pledge interest will <strong>never be silently converted</strong> into a payment or binding commitment.</li><li><strong>Stripe is not active</strong> on GreenlitX. No payment processor currently collects film pledges or membership payments for GreenlitX.</li><li>Supporters are <strong>not investors</strong>. Supporting a project gives no equity, ownership, dividends, revenue share, or financial return.</li><li><strong>Estimated production budgets are not fundraising goals.</strong> Until a campaign goal is expressly set and published for a project, that project&#x27;s goal is <strong>to be determined</strong>.</li><li><strong>Memberships are a separate product</strong> from film pledges. No membership is sold on the basis of interest or a follow.</li></ul>\n<h3>2. How the intended conditional pledge model would work</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX <strong>intends</strong> to offer, in future, a conditional, all-or-nothing pledge model. This Section describes that intention. It is not a description of a live payment system.</p>\n<p>Under the intended model:</p>\n<ol><li>A campaign for a specific project opens with a published campaign goal and a published deadline.</li><li>A supporter chooses an amount and — only at that future time, and only through a separate explicit consent flow — saves a payment method with the payment processor and agrees to one conditional charge.</li><li>Nothing is charged when the pledge is made.</li><li>If total pledges reach <strong>100% of the published campaign goal on or before the deadline</strong>, each supporter is charged <strong>once</strong>, for exactly the amount they pledged.</li><li>If the goal is not reached, nothing is charged (see Section 5).</li><li>Amounts <strong>pledged</strong> (promised) and amounts <strong>collected</strong> (actually received after successful charges) are tracked and displayed as separate figures. Pledged is never presented as collected, and a pledged total is never presented as money in hand.</li></ol>\n<p>Mechanics that are <strong>not yet established</strong> — including how the 100% figure would be treated if some individual charges fail, and the length of any window to fix a failed payment method — must be settled by legal review and published before the model is activated. GreenlitX will not describe an unsettled mechanic to supporters as fact.</p>\n<h3>3. How campaign goals are determined</h3>\n<ul><li>A campaign goal would be set <strong>per project by the owner of GreenlitX</strong> and published on that project&#x27;s page before any payment pledge is accepted for that campaign.</li><li>A campaign goal is <strong>never auto-derived from a project&#x27;s estimated production budget</strong>. A budget is a planning estimate of production cost; a goal is a separate decision.</li><li>Until a goal is set and published for a project, its campaign goal is <strong>to be determined</strong>, and no payment can be charged for that project.</li><li>Whether a published goal would be measured as the gross amount charged or the amount remaining after payment-processing fees is a decision that must be made and disclosed before activation, because processing fees can be a material amount on a large campaign. [Decision to be made by owner with legal and accounting review.]</li></ul>\n<h3>4. When a payment could eventually be charged</h3>\n<p>A payment could eventually be charged <strong>only after all</strong> of the following are in place:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Separate legal readiness</strong> — qualified legal review of the pledge model, the consent wording, consumer disclosures, and the cancellation and refund rules is complete;</li><li><strong>Separate payment readiness</strong> — a payment-processor account is activated and the processor has given any approval it requires for this type of platform; and</li><li><strong>A separate explicit consent flow</strong> — the supporter, for that specific campaign, saves a payment method with the processor at that time and agrees to the exact amount, the published goal, the deadline, and the single conditional charge.</li></ol>\n<p>Nothing recorded before that flow — pledge interest, an account, a follow, or a membership — counts as payment consent, and none of it will be charged retroactively.</p>\n<p>GreenlitX also intends, before any future charge runs, to remind the supporter of the project, the exact amount, and the charge window, and to give them a way to cancel or update their payment method beforehand. [Reminder timing and mechanics to be published before activation.]</p>\n<h3>5. What happens when a goal is not reached</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Nothing is charged.</strong></li><li>There is nothing to refund, because nothing was taken.</li><li>Pledge interest — and, under the intended model, the conditional pledge itself — <strong>lapses</strong> for that campaign. It does not roll over automatically to another project, another campaign, or a later deadline.</li><li>GreenlitX will not substitute a different project for the one a supporter backed.</li></ul>\n<p>While pledges are interest only (today&#x27;s position), a supporter may also withdraw their interest at any time before any future campaign exists. Withdrawal is free.</p>\n<h3>6. Cancellation and refunds — intentions pending legal establishment</h3>\n<p>No pledge payments are taken today, so there are no pledge payments to cancel or refund today.</p>\n<p>For a future payment model, GreenlitX&#x27;s <strong>intentions</strong> — stated as intentions only, pending legal establishment — are:</p>\n<ul><li>A supporter would be able to cancel a conditional pledge free of charge at any time before the campaign goal is reached and the charge runs;</li><li>If GreenlitX cancels a funded project before production funds for that project are released, amounts charged for that project would be refunded in full to the original payment method; and</li><li>All other refund situations would be governed by a final refund policy settled by legal review and published before any payment method is saved for a pledge.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>These are not yet established mechanics and must not be relied on as a binding refund policy.</strong> Matters a final policy must settle include: a production failure after funds have been released, the treatment of processing fees that a processor does not return on a refund, chargeback and dispute handling, and how and when collected funds may be paid out to a production. GreenlitX will not present any of those points as settled until they are.</p>\n<h3>7. How GreenlitX intends to earn revenue</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX&#x27;s intended revenue sources are:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Memberships</strong> — a separate product from film pledges, sold (if and when offered) at a published price with published benefits, billing, and cancellation terms. See the Membership Terms. Membership revenue is GreenlitX revenue; it is not film funding unless a specific purchase page expressly states a specific funding allocation.</li><li><strong>A possible future platform fee on collected campaign funds</strong> — if GreenlitX ever charges one, the fee amount or percentage must be disclosed in the Pledge and Funding Terms and on this page <strong>before</strong> payment activation, and it would apply only as disclosed. No platform fee is charged on pledge interest, and no undisclosed fee will be deducted from any future collection.</li></ul>\n<p>GreenlitX does not earn revenue from selling personal data, and the Privacy Policy states that personal data is not sold or rented and is not shared with advertisers.</p>\n<p>Any future revenue source that affects what a supporter pays or what a project receives must be added to this page before it is introduced.</p>\n<h3>8. What happens if a funded project cannot proceed</h3>\n<p>This Section can only be relevant to a future payment model, because no project is funded through GreenlitX today. It states GreenlitX&#x27;s intentions, pending legal establishment of the final rules in Section 6.</p>\n<p>If, after a future campaign were funded, the project could not proceed:</p>\n<ul><li>The situation would be <strong>disclosed honestly to that project&#x27;s supporters</strong> — GreenlitX will not quietly substitute a different project, reassign the support to another film, or present the original project as proceeding when it is not;</li><li>If production funds for the project had <strong>not yet been released</strong>, GreenlitX&#x27;s intention is that charged amounts for that project would be refunded in full to the original payment method (see Section 6);</li><li>If production funds had <strong>already been released</strong> and spent in whole or in part, a refund of unspent or recovered amounts — and the limits of what can be refunded — would be governed by the final refund policy published before activation. GreenlitX cannot honestly promise in advance that money already spent on a production can be returned, and this page does not make that promise; and</li><li>A funded project would not return to the same supporters for additional pledges for the same film to cover the failure.</li></ul>\n<p>The final version of this Section must be settled by legal review, together with the money-handling structure in Section 9, before any payment is accepted.</p>\n<h3>9. How collected funds would be handled — not yet established</h3>\n<p>GreenlitX does not currently collect, hold, or pay out campaign funds, because charging is not active.</p>\n<p>Before any collection could begin, GreenlitX must establish and disclose — with legal and accounting review — how collected funds would be held, whether an escrow or other segregated arrangement is required and would be used, how long funds may be held before being paid to a production, whether payouts would be milestone-based, and what would happen to collected funds if GreenlitX itself ceased operating. [Structure to be established; no arrangement is claimed in this draft.]</p>\n<p>Until that structure exists and is published here, no campaign may move from pledge interest to payment collection.</p>\n<h3>10. AI, concept materials, and honest status</h3>\n<p>Posters, teasers, and other concept materials — including AI-generated material — illustrate projects in development. They do not establish production activity, financing, casting, or a release.</p>\n<p>Project pages are expected to state each project&#x27;s real development status. If you see a page that appears to overstate a project&#x27;s status, its funding, or what a pledge does today, contact GreenlitX at: GreenlitX — team@greenlitx.com</p>\n<h3>11. Changes to this page</h3>\n<p>We will update this page whenever the pledge model, a campaign-goal rule, a fee, a revenue source, or the payment status of the platform changes. A change to this page will not convert existing pledge interest into a payment — any future charge requires the separate consent flow in Section 4.</p>"}];
